[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366006 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124571 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef311af0-0e95-5574-a151-2971ad66bd08" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Eigenvalues of a 4x4 Matrix Containing a Block of Zeros"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e7e5456c-c3dd-5a0d-b1c2-3704fc8684a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451347} {:answer/id #uuid "2e3a06ba-b64e-5195-8730-c9b460991557" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451348}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e7e5456c-c3dd-5a0d-b1c2-3704fc8684a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451347} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1d9b580-5d43-50e2-927e-f8ae49947f12" :answer-field/key "$\\lambda =2$ is an eigenvalue of $C$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452521} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44f80825-4dc0-5703-964c-52ef15f73d55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451349} {:answer/id #uuid "183ba4dd-4b07-51c8-addc-3f2d2bef0202" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451350}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "183ba4dd-4b07-51c8-addc-3f2d2bef0202" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451350} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e54440b-ec3a-55ca-8152-eddfc8c269f2" :answer-field/key "$\\lambda =5$ is an eigenvalue of $C$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452522} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd8399ad-e6d4-5375-a818-f30592990e92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451351} {:answer/id #uuid "171f1a31-d670-547d-9146-cec5d20385e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451352}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "171f1a31-d670-547d-9146-cec5d20385e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451352} :answer-field/id #uuid "b104c177-2725-5efd-84ea-448298f66c5d" :answer-field/key "The eigenvalue $\\lambda =5$ has algebraic multiplicity $2$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452523}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e41ff16d-c666-5918-91e3-4e187ca2af95" :question/math-academy-id "q-37387" :question/problem "$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 2 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -7 & 6 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $C$ given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the matrix $\\,C-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nC-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}2-\\lambda  & 1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5-\\lambda  & 0 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 2 & -3-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 1 & -7 & 6 & 5-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is $det(C-\\lambda I)=0.$ \n\nNotice that we can expand the $4\\times 4$ determinant across the $4$th column, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(C-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}2-\\lambda  & 1 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5-\\lambda  & 0 & 0 \\\\ -5 & 2 & -3-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 1 & -7 & 6 & 5-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (5-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}2-\\lambda  & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 5-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ -5 & 2 & -3-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, expanding the $3\\times 3$ determinant across the $2$nd row, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5-\\lambda )\\cdot (5-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}2-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ -5 & -3-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (5-\\lambda )^{2}\\cdot ((2-\\lambda )(-3-\\lambda )+20) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -5)^{2}({\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda +14) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $D<0$ for the polynomial ${\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda +14$ we have that the only real eigenvalue is $\\lambda =5$ (with algebraic multiplicity $2$). \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$\\lambda =5$ is an eigenvalue of $C$”; “The eigenvalue $\\lambda =5$ has algebraic multiplicity $2$” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366039 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188273 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e12609b7-286b-50a8-8373-a331d60ab904" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Rank-Nullity Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ffbbdf5b-b5a9-58a5-b789-13db6ff1a8ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469014} {:answer/id #uuid "725a5695-1253-5d33-8d18-e5f71e632c92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469015}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ffbbdf5b-b5a9-58a5-b789-13db6ff1a8ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469014} :answer-field/id #uuid "134c0d8a-c3e3-5d31-a016-da1dea915d49" :answer-field/key "If $\\text{rank}(T)=2,$ then $\\text{Ker}(T)={0}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470119} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c68912f2-a32e-5af0-bfd8-53a4e352fc40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469016} {:answer/id #uuid "90511e22-e82e-54b5-a8be-46168f1b6a40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469017}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "90511e22-e82e-54b5-a8be-46168f1b6a40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469017} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d16e520-7b60-5e2f-8e42-9334e0996e9c" :answer-field/key "If $[-2,0,0]^{T}$ is a solution of $T(x)=0$ then $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470120} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e45344d-c214-5cf1-9c6c-7c83274e9ea5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469018} {:answer/id #uuid "c1ea614b-40b5-59b4-ac19-8dc8a355b72f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469019}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e45344d-c214-5cf1-9c6c-7c83274e9ea5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469018} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f93777f-8292-5756-b7f9-4905592e30dc" :answer-field/key "If $T(x)=[1,0,0]^{T}$ has no solution then $\\text{rank}(T)=3$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470121}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "006b1b75-818e-5ba9-8f81-bc35ede44097" :question/math-academy-id "q-38141" :question/problem "Let $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,\\left\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}\\right.$ \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T)=3.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “If $\\text{rank}(T)=2,$ then $\\text{Ker}(T)={0}$” is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =3-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}.$\n\n- Statement “If $[-2,0,0]^{T}$ is a solution of $T(x)=0$ then $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0$” is true. Since $\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ is a solution of $Tx=0$, we have that $\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\in \\text{Ker}(T)$ and therefore $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement “If $T(x)=[1,0,0]^{T}$ has no solution then $\\text{rank}(T)=3$” is false. Since $T(x)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ has no solution, we have that $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{3}.$ Hence, $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct option is “If $[-2,0,0]^{T}$ is a solution of $T(x)=0$ then $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0$” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366052 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176089 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba9d2401-4e30-5327-b8eb-b4db7108dd0c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Linear Transformations in the Cartesian Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d07d408-4bd4-52a2-b369-1432edfe2c77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469020} {:answer/id #uuid "eaeb8c45-a223-54ca-b996-e4cc9a54fe8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469021}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6d07d408-4bd4-52a2-b369-1432edfe2c77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469020} :answer-field/id #uuid "246b7ed5-a7fd-53be-80d4-b0d726f0fda5" :answer-field/key "$⟨1,3⟩$ belongs to the range of $T$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470122} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f53549c-840d-53f3-bf45-f0d90bcc6ab3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469022} {:answer/id #uuid "215f631a-fd6a-5cc0-906f-13e0a5fbd25e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469023}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "215f631a-fd6a-5cc0-906f-13e0a5fbd25e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469023} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a6bf326-d27f-5130-ad2f-4b066b4f2754" :answer-field/key "$\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470123} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5bb3b5e2-0da0-57b0-a807-697b0aeddec8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469024} {:answer/id #uuid "a8fd395f-d3c4-53ed-b990-698626ffd03e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469025}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5bb3b5e2-0da0-57b0-a807-697b0aeddec8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469024} :answer-field/id #uuid "435f823d-9228-557b-a231-fc7e35bab06c" :answer-field/key "$\\text{Im}(T)={0}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470124}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "34581764-4087-5b06-9eff-4679fe703405" :question/math-academy-id "q-38300" :question/problem "If the linear transformation $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto the line $y=-3x$ then, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that when the image of a linear transformation $T$ in ${R}^{2}$ with the standard matrix $T$ is a one-dimensional line, then\n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)=1\n$$\n\nand, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{nullity}(T) & =2-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn. \n\n- Statement “$⟨1,3⟩$ belongs to the range of $T$” is false. \nNotice that the point $(1,3)$ does not satisfy the equation of the line $y=-3x$ since $3\\ne -3\\cdot 1.$ Therefore, the vector $⟨1,3⟩\\notin \\text{Im}(T).$\n\n- Statement $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}$ is true. Indeed, \nsince $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto a line passing through the origin, we have that the standard matrix $T$ is singular. Hence, $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}.$\n\n- Statement $\\text{Im}(T)={0}$ is false. \nFor example,  the point $(1,-3)$ satisfies the equation  $y=-3x$ since $-3=-3\\cdot 1.$ Therefore, the vector $⟨1,-3⟩\\in \\text{Im}(T).$ Thus, $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {0}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366059 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176089 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba9d2401-4e30-5327-b8eb-b4db7108dd0c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Linear Transformations in the Cartesian Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cf5d61a3-731b-5ca2-a21d-a258c3b1767d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469026} {:answer/id #uuid "83d5e69a-fb13-5cb4-8279-35f40cd14786" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469027}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf5d61a3-731b-5ca2-a21d-a258c3b1767d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469026} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad7fbb88-7ce0-52f3-bfca-0238dcd3a81c" :answer-field/key "$⟨\\frac{6}{5},1⟩\\in \\text{Im}(T)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470125} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94b0ff15-d102-5f1d-af4e-e362a8a9b9a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469028} {:answer/id #uuid "c0529496-06cf-5552-ac7c-13815e3edd61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469029}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c0529496-06cf-5552-ac7c-13815e3edd61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469029} :answer-field/id #uuid "990ff885-053e-5892-a630-17d9382e4120" :answer-field/key "$\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470126} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e46a229c-3d63-5185-b98f-3bcb74c8a528" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469030} {:answer/id #uuid "c12af7cf-9477-5a53-a812-9a97f8176c74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469031}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e46a229c-3d63-5185-b98f-3bcb74c8a528" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469030} :answer-field/id #uuid "4bce9e44-27f0-518d-ad2a-de5a2a028195" :answer-field/key "$\\text{nullity}(T)=0$, where $T$ is the standard matrix of $T$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470127}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "07bd3910-da00-5269-ba42-c4e894fc0a77" :question/math-academy-id "q-38305" :question/problem "If the linear transformation $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto the line $5y=6x$ then, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that when the image of a linear transformation $T$ in ${R}^{2}$ with the standard matrix $T$ is a one-dimensional line, then\n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)=1\n$$\n\nand, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{nullity}(T) & =2-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn. \n\n- Statement $⟨\\frac{6}{5},1⟩\\in \\text{Im}(T)$ is false.\nNotice that the point $(\\frac{6}{5},1)$ does not satisfy the equation of the line $5y=6x$ since $5\\cdot 1\\ne \\frac{6}{5}\\cdot 6.$ Therefore, \n$⟨\\frac{6}{5},1⟩\\notin \\text{Im}(T).$\n\n- Statement $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}$ is true.\nSince $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto a line passing through the origin, we have that the standard matrix $T$ is singular. So, $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}.$\n\n- Statement “$\\text{nullity}(T)=0$, where $T$ is the standard matrix of $T$” is false.\nSince  statement $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}$ is true we have that $\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{dim(Ker}(T))\\ne 0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366097 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188273 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e12609b7-286b-50a8-8373-a331d60ab904" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Rank-Nullity Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e4e29d4-8ab4-51b2-bed7-3d933724288f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469038} {:answer/id #uuid "366b643d-0477-56b8-99e8-05665c95ecc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469039}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e4e29d4-8ab4-51b2-bed7-3d933724288f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469038} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa806480-98e3-5de9-ac37-45bb7e60daee" :answer-field/key "If $\\text{Ker}(T)={⟨x,y,z⟩\\,|\\,x+y=0}$ then $\\text{rank}(T)+1=n$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470131} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0dba33dd-efd6-5edd-8cb4-431f80150830" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469040} {:answer/id #uuid "03dbdbf2-dd97-5434-bd34-a6b20449669a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469041}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0dba33dd-efd6-5edd-8cb4-431f80150830" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469040} :answer-field/id #uuid "bad0d392-5687-58fc-8043-83455bbb6881" :answer-field/key "If there exists a vector $v\\notin \\text{Im}(T),$ then $|T|\\ne 0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470132} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c5c5fde-9d69-5dd0-bd3e-c686ea921e45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469042} {:answer/id #uuid "c12380e7-7519-5b6e-8745-f0b879e1df9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469043}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c5c5fde-9d69-5dd0-bd3e-c686ea921e45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469042} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e9fc1cb-34d2-5ac7-b925-bd6d7335fddb" :answer-field/key "If ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ is linearly independent and $T({v}_{1})=T({v}_{2})=0$ then $\\text{nullity}(T)=1$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470133}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "936f721f-93be-5c51-8233-7da49b500101" :question/math-academy-id "q-38979" :question/problem "Let $T:{R}^{n}\\to {R}^{n}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{n}$ into ${R}^{n},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,\\left\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{n} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=n \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}\\right.$ \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T)=n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “If $\\text{Ker}(T)={⟨x,y,z⟩\\,|\\,x+y=0}$ then $\\text{rank}(T)+1=n$” is false. Solving $x+y=0$ for $x,$ we get $x=-y.$ Therefore, any vector from $\\text{Im}(T)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-y \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=y\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{{v}_{1}}+z\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{v}_{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}.$ Therefore, $\\text{nullity}(T)=2.$ Finally, according the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T) & =n \\\\ \\text{rank}(T)+2 & =n.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “If there exists a vector $v\\notin \\text{Im}(T),$ then $|T|\\ne 0$” is false. If $v\\notin \\text{Im}(T)$ then $\\text{Im}(T)\\ne {R}^{n}$, so, according to the invertible matrix theorem, $T$ is not a bijection. Thus, the matrix $T$ is not invertible, and, consequently, $|T|=0.$\n\n- Statement “If ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ is linearly independent and $T({v}_{1})=T({v}_{2})=0$ then $\\text{nullity}(T)=1$” is false. Since $T({v}_{1})=T({v}_{2})=0$, then ${v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\in \\text{Ker}(T).$ But the vectors are linearly independent, therefore, the dimension of  $\\text{Ker}(T)$ is at least $2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366219 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147427 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a19140af-1870-5dee-86a2-6052451803d4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Intersection of Two Planes"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cfbaf102-c0df-5e93-9fdd-76ff8e170914" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451507} {:answer/id #uuid "bd856043-9bf5-545a-9dab-355b675c30de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451508}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cfbaf102-c0df-5e93-9fdd-76ff8e170914" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451507} :answer-field/id #uuid "17379ff6-7738-574d-b3f4-b367ff0dfb86" :answer-field/key "The normal vectors of the planes are parallel." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452601} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eacdbb44-ca0c-548a-9e83-9c47d872f78f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451509} {:answer/id #uuid "f26307df-edf9-5e2f-ae55-61a18a228540" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451510}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eacdbb44-ca0c-548a-9e83-9c47d872f78f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451509} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1aea80c-069f-5199-b8cf-083b0c70f9f9" :answer-field/key "The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $0.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452602} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c5d27925-4f17-57c7-83c0-083d924358a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451511} {:answer/id #uuid "b187a096-71e0-5c74-af3c-85207909140b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451512}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b187a096-71e0-5c74-af3c-85207909140b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451512} :answer-field/id #uuid "22f7c0e5-f396-5066-adbe-dbe1244d165d" :answer-field/key "The planes intersect on a line." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452603}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "96c9a429-d310-55eb-845b-95fc86ca78d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-71448" :question/problem "Given the two planes $x-2y+z=0$ and $x-y+1=0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that:\n\n- For the plane $x-2y+z=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=⟨1,-2,1⟩.$\n\n- For the plane $x-y+1=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=⟨1,-1,0⟩.$\n\nNow, let's consider the statements.\n\n- Statements “The normal vectors of the planes are parallel” and “The cross product of the normal vectors of the planes is equal to $0.$” are false. Indeed, computing the cross product of the normal vectors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} & =⟨1,-2,1⟩\\times ⟨1,-1,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =i+j+k \\\\  & =⟨1,1,1⟩\\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2}\\ne 0$, the vectors ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are not parallel.\n\n- Statement “The planes intersect on a line” is true. Indeed, two non-parallel planes in $3$-dimensional space must intersect on a line.\n\nTherefore, only statement “The planes intersect on a line” is true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366365 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124571 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef311af0-0e95-5574-a151-2971ad66bd08" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Eigenvalues of a 4x4 Matrix Containing a Block of Zeros"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86ec9910-e0fd-500c-a549-944d8205800e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451789} {:answer/id #uuid "5a37d4f9-58db-5ba8-88b3-85410ad5ea6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451790}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a37d4f9-58db-5ba8-88b3-85410ad5ea6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451790} :answer-field/id #uuid "d02090ef-d4ce-52dc-a572-f7d577aef6d1" :answer-field/key "$\\lambda =0$ is an eigenvalue of $P$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452742} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5bd5e478-5cfa-5b4b-b744-a3677754f14d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451791} {:answer/id #uuid "0b89ae10-1176-5228-9818-87068f6baeb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451792}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0b89ae10-1176-5228-9818-87068f6baeb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451792} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f49c755-7208-571d-b62b-fa250286bf1d" :answer-field/key "$\\lambda =-14$ is an eigenvalue of $P$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452743} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d81aeff-94a5-5d39-a36b-e4ef94203480" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451793} {:answer/id #uuid "1923b6cb-681f-5678-aa63-c64a0628a021" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451794}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d81aeff-94a5-5d39-a36b-e4ef94203480" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451793} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba776b6a-55ef-5233-8b4a-47f605e790d5" :answer-field/key "The eigenvalue $\\lambda =2$ has algebraic multiplicity $2$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452744}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c6753a04-a08e-5bc2-a11a-2cad03fb8d3f" :question/math-academy-id "q-80800" :question/problem "$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -3 & -8 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -2 & 11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -14\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $P$ given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the matrix $\\,P-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nP-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}2-\\lambda  & -3 & -8 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -\\lambda  & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -2-\\lambda  & 11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -14-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is $det(P-\\lambda I)=0.$ \n\nNotice that we can expand the $4\\times 4$ determinant across the $1$st column, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(P-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}2-\\lambda  & -3 & -8 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -\\lambda  & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -2-\\lambda  & 11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -14-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}-\\lambda  & 0 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -2-\\lambda  & 11 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -14-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, expanding the $3\\times 3$ determinant across the $3$rd row, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2-\\lambda )\\cdot (-14-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}-\\lambda  & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(-14-\\lambda )(-\\lambda )(-2-\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)(\\lambda +14)\\lambda (\\lambda +2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues of $P$ are $\\lambda =2,$ $\\lambda =-2,$ $\\lambda =0,$ and $\\lambda =-14$  (all with algebraic multiplicity $1$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$\\lambda =0$ is an eigenvalue of $P$”; “$\\lambda =-14$ is an eigenvalue of $P$” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366427 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188273 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e12609b7-286b-50a8-8373-a331d60ab904" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Rank-Nullity Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b6b563a-739c-5aa6-aa3d-91cf2bda7eee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469302} {:answer/id #uuid "0ae5e117-a505-5a04-81e4-439b74052c17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469303}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ae5e117-a505-5a04-81e4-439b74052c17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469303} :answer-field/id #uuid "0829ea20-3a9e-5bac-9aac-a20a5031708f" :answer-field/key "$\\text{nullity}(T)=2$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470263} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "86ea0788-dc9f-5b43-b4d9-3e6d92f4a3f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469304} {:answer/id #uuid "16e976b2-1f3b-5daf-bc6f-ee9f3ff92e50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469305}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16e976b2-1f3b-5daf-bc6f-ee9f3ff92e50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469305} :answer-field/id #uuid "55766021-6ec3-5fd5-927e-a3d5966052e8" :answer-field/key "$\\text{rank}(T)=1$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470264} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "584ceae8-0e69-5969-b025-a8dfde1e4426" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469306} {:answer/id #uuid "24a75a78-d9f9-590b-98ed-a75a55f1195e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469307}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "584ceae8-0e69-5969-b025-a8dfde1e4426" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469306} :answer-field/id #uuid "44fe36b7-f90a-541a-a159-56cfdbf08c21" :answer-field/key "$T$ is a bijection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470265}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3f6a64ec-417d-5f3c-a079-819cbc656ca3" :question/math-academy-id "q-83440" :question/problem "Let $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T,$ and let ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ be a set of linearly independent vectors in ${R}^{3}.$ Consider the following statement:\n\n*If  $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$, then $\\underline{\\phantom{xxxxxx}}.$ *\n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,\\left\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}\\right.$ \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T)=3.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement $\\text{nullity}(T)=2$ is true. Since $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ and the set ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}$ is linearly independent, we get that $\\text{nullity}(T)=2.$\n\n- Statement $\\text{rank}(T)=1$ is true. According to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(T) & =3-\\text{nullity}(T) \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$T$ is a bijection” is false. Since $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 3$ (or since $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0$), the linear transformation $T$ cannot be a bijection according to the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, the correct option is $\\text{nullity}(T)=2$; $\\text{rank}(T)=1$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366455 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188273 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e12609b7-286b-50a8-8373-a331d60ab904" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Using the Rank-Nullity Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07f285c5-f28d-52d7-8edb-dc3cd9308f5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451845} {:answer/id #uuid "de316b3c-8465-5f0f-9e1d-c901b94e2cdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451846}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de316b3c-8465-5f0f-9e1d-c901b94e2cdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451846} :answer-field/id #uuid "0bbe03ec-cd1a-5f9e-bdaa-621344b8730a" :answer-field/key "If $\\text{Im}(T)={[x,y,z]^{T}\\,:\\,x=0}$ then $\\text{nullity}(T)=1$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452770} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c2821d1-dbdf-57db-8669-559772eabf32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451847} {:answer/id #uuid "0c7b316c-fd27-57d7-ae6d-1a8dea1ee57f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451848}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c7b316c-fd27-57d7-ae6d-1a8dea1ee57f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451848} :answer-field/id #uuid "ecdec4e8-476e-5e64-bfe1-002886cf49b8" :answer-field/key "If $\\begin{gathered}\\text{Ker}(T)=\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}\\,[x,y,z]^{T}\\,:\\\\\\,y-2z=0,\\\\x+z=0\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$ then $\\text{rank}(T)=2$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452771} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e26320c-f5fc-5d22-962b-bc6fcc8e7c1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451849} {:answer/id #uuid "fd978c93-8391-56ee-b944-a6b07dbbf21d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451850}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd978c93-8391-56ee-b944-a6b07dbbf21d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451850} :answer-field/id #uuid "38dc4bf6-46bb-5bfd-8850-b313313b3744" :answer-field/key "If ${v}_{1},$ ${v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are the columns of $T$ and ${v}_{3}={v}_{1}-{v}_{2}$, then $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452772}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c81c0d34-9745-5e75-9229-e0ad0c633344" :question/math-academy-id "q-84063" :question/problem "Let $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,\\left\\{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}\\right.$ \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T)=3.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement “If $\\text{Im}(T)={[x,y,z]^{T}\\,:\\,x=0}$ then $\\text{nullity}(T)=1$” is true. Any vector from $\\text{Im}(T)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=y\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}}_{{v}_{1}}+z\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{v}_{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}.$ Therefore, $\\text{rank}(T)=2.$ Finally, according the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =3-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “If $\\begin{gathered}\\text{Ker}(T)=\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}\\,[x,y,z]^{T}\\,:\\\\\\,y-2z=0,\\\\x+z=0\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$ then $\\text{rank}(T)=2$” is true. Solving $y-2z=0$ for $y,$ we get $y=2z.$ Solving $x+z=0$ for $x,$ we get $x=-z.$ Therefore, any vector from $\\text{Im}(T)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-z \\\\ 2z \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=z\\underbrace{\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}}_{{v}_{1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1}}.$ Therefore, $\\text{nullity}(T)=1.$ Finally, according the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(T) & =3-\\text{nullity}(T) \\\\  & =3-1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “If ${v}_{1},$ ${v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are the columns of $T$ and ${v}_{3}={v}_{1}-{v}_{2}$, then $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}$” is true. If the columns of $T$ are such that ${v}_{3}={v}_{1}-{v}_{2},$ then the set of vectors ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly dependent and therefore $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Col}(T)\\ne {R}^{3}.$ Now, according to the matrix invertible theorem, we obtain that $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “If $\\text{Im}(T)={[x,y,z]^{T}\\,:\\,x=0}$ then $\\text{nullity}(T)=1$”; “If $\\begin{gathered}\\text{Ker}(T)=\\left\\{\\begin{gathered}\\,[x,y,z]^{T}\\,:\\\\\\,y-2z=0,\\\\x+z=0\\end{gathered}\\right\\}\\end{gathered}$ then $\\text{rank}(T)=2$”; “If ${v}_{1},$ ${v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are the columns of $T$ and ${v}_{3}={v}_{1}-{v}_{2}$, then $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}$”."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366483 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133951 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "da20ffe7-173b-5fc0-8629-3b9b70222860" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Orthogonal Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7b65968b-6028-551a-9631-58b67db16afa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469374} {:answer/id #uuid "a75e0f1b-240f-5f97-b88c-017b10166d2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469375}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b65968b-6028-551a-9631-58b67db16afa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469374} :answer-field/id #uuid "b7a2f7a7-5e01-577f-9f82-f523dd836563" :answer-field/key "$2PQ$ is always orthogonal" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470299} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c8af276a-607f-58cb-95a3-dfb795f49d12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469376} {:answer/id #uuid "de04d0f8-5260-5b89-a3cd-b666a82c85fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469377}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c8af276a-607f-58cb-95a3-dfb795f49d12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469376} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d01531c-c3a3-5572-8137-9d105c60ab89" :answer-field/key "$P-Q$ is always orthogonal" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470300} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0feb765d-c901-5436-a1e5-1524e2608115" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469378} {:answer/id #uuid "aadde301-7372-5694-8cca-d9b6c7b72bd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469379}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0feb765d-c901-5436-a1e5-1524e2608115" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469378} :answer-field/id #uuid "e169f11f-de5b-5f0d-ad57-d18c3a6c238d" :answer-field/key "$P$ is **not** invertible" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470301}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e6401598-f82b-58db-b408-23496dde6519" :question/math-academy-id "q-84344" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the $n\\times n$ orthogonal matrices $P$ and $Q?$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $Q$ is orthogonal if and only if\n\n$$\nQ{Q}^{T}={Q}^{T}Q=I.\n$$\n\nTo check that a matrix is orthogonal, it is sufficient to check that the above equation is satisfied.\n\nMoreover, we have the following properties:\n\n- For any orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we have ${Q}^{-1}={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $(AB)^{T}={B}^{T}{A}^{T}$ for conformable matrices $A$ and $B.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “$2PQ$ is always orthogonal” is false. Computing the product $(2PQ)\\cdot (2PQ)^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2PQ)\\cdot (2PQ)^{T} & =2PQ\\cdot 2{Q}^{T}{P}^{T} \\\\  & =4P(Q{Q}^{T}){P}^{T} \\\\  & =4P(I){P}^{T} \\\\  & =4I \\\\  & \\ne I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$P-Q$ is always orthogonal” is false. As a counterexample, consider the following subtraction of two orthogonal matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe resulting matrix is not orthogonal since, for example, its first column is not normalized.\n\n- Statement “$P$ is **not** invertible” is false. The inverse of an orthogonal matrix $P$ is given by ${P}^{-1}={P}^{T}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true\"."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366490 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133951 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "da20ffe7-173b-5fc0-8629-3b9b70222860" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements About Orthogonal Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a454a558-e9c9-559c-8c56-52e733918130" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469380} {:answer/id #uuid "93599e5b-ce93-5b16-a5b8-85ac566f908e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469381}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "93599e5b-ce93-5b16-a5b8-85ac566f908e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469381} :answer-field/id #uuid "08b21958-5070-57d3-ab7f-c8d4fa998b69" :answer-field/key "$Q$ is invertible" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470302} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "861675fc-dffe-5e49-8d64-223921d9e84a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469382} {:answer/id #uuid "e927ecab-f475-5bf8-84b4-be36a9c65426" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469383}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e927ecab-f475-5bf8-84b4-be36a9c65426" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469383} :answer-field/id #uuid "600fda14-a171-59e1-b8a6-d69e1c29474d" :answer-field/key "${P}^{T}$ is orthogonal" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470303} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d0a2b49-a427-56fc-9dbd-1ad4ffd42ff4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469384} {:answer/id #uuid "a9ef53d4-cbb1-5d21-b743-dd86c3da732c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469385}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9ef53d4-cbb1-5d21-b743-dd86c3da732c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469385} :answer-field/id #uuid "546258ed-c776-5904-bc1f-596ecebf3ec5" :answer-field/key "${Q}^{-1}$ is always orthogonal" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470304}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "dcdd2917-752d-5fd1-ac2a-6a285cbe6a4f" :question/math-academy-id "q-84353" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the $n\\times n$ orthogonal matrices $P$ and $Q?$\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Recall that a matrix $Q$ is orthogonal if and only if\n\n$$\nQ{Q}^{T}={Q}^{T}Q=I.\n$$\n\nTo check that a matrix is orthogonal, it is sufficient to check that the above equation is satisfied.\n\nMoreover, we have the following properties:\n\n- For any orthogonal matrix $Q,$ we have ${Q}^{-1}={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $(AB)^{T}={B}^{T}{A}^{T}$ for conformable matrices $A$ and $B.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “$Q$ is invertible” is true. The inverse of an orthogonal matrix $Q$ is given by ${Q}^{-1}={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- Statement “${P}^{T}$ is orthogonal” is true. Computing the product ${P}^{T}\\cdot ({P}^{T})^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{T}\\cdot ({P}^{T})^{T} & ={P}^{T}\\cdot P \\\\  & =I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “${Q}^{-1}$ is always orthogonal” is true. Computing the product ${Q}^{-1}\\cdot ({Q}^{-1})^{T},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Q}^{-1}\\cdot ({Q}^{-1})^{T} & ={Q}^{T}\\cdot ({Q}^{T})^{T} \\\\  & ={Q}^{T}Q \\\\  & =I.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$Q$ is invertible”; “${P}^{T}$ is orthogonal”; “${Q}^{-1}$ is always orthogonal”."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366511 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124571 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef311af0-0e95-5574-a151-2971ad66bd08" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Eigenvalues of a 4x4 Matrix Containing a Block of Zeros"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d3139a9d-4709-52dc-b082-fa6e7262b4f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451863} {:answer/id #uuid "6611bee2-086e-5458-82d6-29162e06ef7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451864}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6611bee2-086e-5458-82d6-29162e06ef7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451864} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b3c705f-a12e-5ec7-9d9e-7cfb964eab33" :answer-field/key "$\\lambda =2$ is an eigenvalue of $D$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452779} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80fd8012-3fa3-5669-b4d0-1a4677a6674d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451865} {:answer/id #uuid "f4d74f60-32b7-5c05-af9d-16a41f64d1c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451866}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f4d74f60-32b7-5c05-af9d-16a41f64d1c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451866} :answer-field/id #uuid "061f5f16-a1a6-5a40-b334-2790c7680235" :answer-field/key "$\\lambda =-1$ is an eigenvalue of $D$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452780} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ceb7b4d4-53de-5934-8e1a-b0b8206b39b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451867} {:answer/id #uuid "3f9d8d40-8af7-502f-800e-af1a162fcfc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451868}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3f9d8d40-8af7-502f-800e-af1a162fcfc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186451868} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5bb764e-2d9e-5196-a9d1-56181fe324ba" :answer-field/key "The eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ has algebraic multiplicity $2$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452781}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5ad99f7f-a225-5eb9-a085-4dc337f7e2b1" :question/math-academy-id "q-86752" :question/problem "$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -2 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -7\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $D$ given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the matrix $\\,D-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nD-\\lambda I=\\begin{bmatrix}-1-\\lambda  & -2 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1-\\lambda  & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -\\lambda  & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -7-\\lambda \\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is $det(D-\\lambda I)=0.$ \n\nNotice that we can expand the $4\\times 4$ determinant across the $1$st column, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(D-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}-1-\\lambda  & -2 & 4 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1-\\lambda  & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -\\lambda  & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & -7-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & -1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & -\\lambda  & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -7-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, expanding the $3\\times 3$ determinant across the $3$rd row, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1-\\lambda )(-7-\\lambda )\\begin{vmatrix}1-\\lambda  & -1 \\\\ -2 & -\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )(-7-\\lambda )((1-\\lambda )(-\\lambda )-2) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)(\\lambda +7)({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -2) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)(\\lambda +7)(\\lambda -2)(\\lambda +1) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)^{2}(\\lambda +7)(\\lambda -2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues of $D$ are $\\lambda =-1$ (with algebraic multiplicity $2$), $\\lambda =-7$ and $\\lambda =2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$\\lambda =2$ is an eigenvalue of $D$”; “$\\lambda =-1$ is an eigenvalue of $D$”; “The eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1$ has algebraic multiplicity $2$”."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366553 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161979 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c2da07e-23b5-58d0-aeef-14e21b329368" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Symmetric Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9295980c-7c10-56c7-9256-1029bff3fede" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469644} {:answer/id #uuid "2f281361-566f-564e-90f3-dc8456c7c329" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469645}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2f281361-566f-564e-90f3-dc8456c7c329" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469645} :answer-field/id #uuid "24260372-e2e3-556d-a806-f1f44e080cec" :answer-field/key "$P=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470434} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "579254ab-eaa2-510b-8f52-1e585f11c777" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469646} {:answer/id #uuid "15fc8fd0-d708-5fc9-8945-923588741ef7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469647}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "15fc8fd0-d708-5fc9-8945-923588741ef7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469647} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2923d22-92c3-5826-8e93-69df978fa286" :answer-field/key "$Q=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 0.5 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0.5 & 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470435} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0273c12c-e70d-5a16-93e2-dabca0aa2e62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469648} {:answer/id #uuid "3790c260-bda0-5fd6-a07c-35ad33e66535" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469649}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0273c12c-e70d-5a16-93e2-dabca0aa2e62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469648} :answer-field/id #uuid "b418d0be-8006-5748-bc6f-012ee528be28" :answer-field/key "$R=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470436}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1c1ef725-7db0-58fe-a7db-0097c019b6b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-93987" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $P={P}^{T}.$\n\n- $Q$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $Q={Q}^{T}.$\n\n- $R$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${r}_{12}\\ne {r}_{21},$ we obtain $R\\ne {R}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nR=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P$ and $Q$ only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366560 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161979 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5c2da07e-23b5-58d0-aeef-14e21b329368" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Symmetric Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0860eddb-8872-5a14-90cd-ea80f80450d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469656} {:answer/id #uuid "a9503884-f719-56eb-b3d2-1bbf12f445b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469657}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9503884-f719-56eb-b3d2-1bbf12f445b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469657} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ceb7b39-d9d7-59a0-905c-bc078aea9c08" :answer-field/key "$P=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470440} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0316227b-bc46-5340-873c-fb4bdb297900" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469658} {:answer/id #uuid "73b30d62-76b6-5783-bbcb-a165c097c0d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469659}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0316227b-bc46-5340-873c-fb4bdb297900" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469658} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0a912f5-f285-5920-8fe7-d7356920694f" :answer-field/key "$Q=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470441} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36ff6b0b-7b61-5a1d-b03e-95adb11dc3a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469660} {:answer/id #uuid "8c62d2d7-9891-554e-bbb7-c20ffae1d6d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469661}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "36ff6b0b-7b61-5a1d-b03e-95adb11dc3a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469660} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab17f333-c773-595b-b978-1a56da303bcf" :answer-field/key "$R=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470442}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a97d726e-d379-5097-84d8-abb6b0a769af" :question/math-academy-id "q-93992" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are symmetric?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal. \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each matrix in turn.\n\n- $P$ is a symmetric matrix. Indeed, $P={P}^{T}.$\n\n- $Q$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${q}_{23}\\ne {q}_{32},$ we obtain $Q\\ne {Q}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nQ=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- $R$ is *not* a symmetric matrix. Since ${r}_{12}\\ne {r}_{21},$ we obtain $R\\ne {R}^{T}.$\n\n$$\nR=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$P$ only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366608 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152221 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e609aabf-7e7e-5997-b8dc-c66e7a14a36f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Finite and Infinite-Dimensional Vector Spaces"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c35f8969-6bb9-503c-b423-baf9ba63f251" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469794} {:answer/id #uuid "f2ac1f94-ccb4-5a6b-ba7f-e5e557bfddf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469795}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c35f8969-6bb9-503c-b423-baf9ba63f251" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469794} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c445df9-5547-539a-8b68-d1638570ff4a" :answer-field/key "$D(R),$ the vector space of all differentiable functions on $R$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470509} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b7c7aed-2927-5e90-a205-e235a342c35b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469796} {:answer/id #uuid "2bed5c08-7e4a-5683-bf39-316bd5100fc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469797}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2bed5c08-7e4a-5683-bf39-316bd5100fc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469797} :answer-field/id #uuid "d396cbe4-dbaa-5006-8ccf-4a8aadfb8964" :answer-field/key "${\\text{M}}_{10\\times 100}(R),$ the vector space of all $10\\times 100$ matrices over $R$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470510} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d963f76c-6988-585e-a71b-75d8f8601841" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469798} {:answer/id #uuid "9f964fca-b809-5920-9514-190c5687344b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469799}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f964fca-b809-5920-9514-190c5687344b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469799} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4bf78bd-6326-5679-9a2d-eb9d8351ce04" :answer-field/key "${R}_{200}[t],$ the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $200$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470511}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bd51b750-1389-5155-b65d-c0946321f8d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-95388" :question/problem "Which of the following vector spaces are finite-dimensional?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine our vector spaces.\n\n- $D(R)$ is *not* finite-dimensional. To understand why, we first note that the following subset of $D(R)$ is linearly independent:\n\n$$\n{1,\\,t,\\,{t}^{2},\\,…,{t}^{n}},\\,n\\in N.\n$$\n\nIndeed, we have that \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n}{x}_{i}{t}^{i}=0\n$$\n\nfor all $t\\in R$ if and only if ${x}_{i}=0$ for all $i=0,1,2,…,n.$ Therefore,  for any  $n\\in N,$  there exists a linearly independent subset of $D(R)$  of size $n,$ which imples that $D(R)$ cannot have finite dimension.     .\n\n- ${\\text{M}}_{10\\times 100}(R)$ and ${R}_{200}[t]$ are finite-dimensional. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{10\\times 100}(R))=10\\cdot 100=1\\,000\n$$\n\n \nand\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}_{200}[t])=200+1=201,\n$$\n\nare both finite numbers.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “${\\text{M}}_{10\\times 100}(R),$ the vector space of all $10\\times 100$ matrices over $R$”; “${R}_{200}[t],$ the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $200$” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366636 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162013 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c67d532-acd9-5231-a27f-d39084863e01" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Spectral Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c6e59cf-eefe-5e7d-9e31-a8784bb64fdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452197} {:answer/id #uuid "21e11fcb-04eb-552c-a533-7cc128693eb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452198}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "21e11fcb-04eb-552c-a533-7cc128693eb7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452198} :answer-field/id #uuid "99c5561a-1c1f-5380-a23d-ed36c45bd3f7" :answer-field/key "$A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452946} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "73d0b8cb-9801-5fc9-a55c-c637e3fd779a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452199} {:answer/id #uuid "f773140f-7c60-560e-8d94-6114e21180eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452200}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "73d0b8cb-9801-5fc9-a55c-c637e3fd779a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452199} :answer-field/id #uuid "b81448bd-7bd9-5f9c-a66a-2e21a29d3fec" :answer-field/key "$A$ must have $8$ real and distinct eigenvalues" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452947} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "73bd6dbf-2be9-5c2b-8fad-7080e8da1057" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452201} {:answer/id #uuid "a2c1e16b-b961-57ac-90e1-29a223838bbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452202}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a2c1e16b-b961-57ac-90e1-29a223838bbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452202} :answer-field/id #uuid "c352f51f-c826-5af3-a439-3bb54f1ba94c" :answer-field/key "If $u$ and $v$ are two eigenvectors that correspond to different eigenvalues, then $u⊥v$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452948}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "66a9cbb1-7460-5af7-96d5-cd936a2ef154" :question/math-academy-id "q-95405" :question/problem "Given that $A$ is an $8\\times 8$ real symmetric matrix, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Given that $A$ is an $n\\times n$ symmetric matrix over $R$, the spectral theorem states the following:\n\n- The eigenvalues of $A$ are all real.\n\n- Eigenvectors corresponding to different eigenvalues are orthogonal.\n\n- The dimension of the eigenspace for each eigenvalue equals the multiplicity of that eigenvalue as a root of the characteristic polynomial.\n\n- $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “$A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable” is true. Indeed, any symmetric matrix with real entries is orthogonally diagonalizable.\n\n- Statement “$A$ must have $8$ real and distinct eigenvalues” is false. According to the spectral theorem, the eigenvalues are real but not necessarily distinct. This occurs when the characteristic polynomial has roots whose (algebraic) multiplicity is greater than $1.$\n\n- Statement “If $u$ and $v$ are two eigenvectors that correspond to different eigenvalues, then $u⊥v$” is true. Indeed, eigenvectors corresponding to different eigenvalues must be orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable”; “If $u$ and $v$ are two eigenvectors that correspond to different eigenvalues, then $u⊥v$” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366643 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162013 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c67d532-acd9-5231-a27f-d39084863e01" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Spectral Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82cbba1a-f069-5a33-a2d1-b502c99b2b3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452203} {:answer/id #uuid "dc6ac186-7f49-5faa-93e6-ca668251994b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452204}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82cbba1a-f069-5a33-a2d1-b502c99b2b3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452203} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e0776b3-44ee-59b9-afc3-45d560491387" :answer-field/key "$A$ is not diagonalizable" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452949} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d482a9a1-475e-5fcc-bafe-deb4762a9d2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452205} {:answer/id #uuid "54115a6d-0ff0-5c69-9194-8cdd62d05b82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452206}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54115a6d-0ff0-5c69-9194-8cdd62d05b82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452206} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4206f3a-3ee1-5441-9ffb-b57c2618eba7" :answer-field/key "If $u$ and $v$ are two eigenvectors that correspond to different eigenvalues, then $u⊥v$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452950} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3455c3f3-d151-520c-b72e-9874313639d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452207} {:answer/id #uuid "69ca346c-3af4-5c9e-91d8-6637dc8c1dbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452208}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69ca346c-3af4-5c9e-91d8-6637dc8c1dbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452208} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa13589e-7777-52d2-8d09-0d1f83bbcf12" :answer-field/key "$A$ must have $4$ real (not necessarily distinct) eigenvalues" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452951}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "42059a97-8b39-59cb-bae6-eab5799ef586" :question/math-academy-id "q-95409" :question/problem "Given that $A$ is a $4\\times 4$ real symmetric matrix, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Given that $A$ is an $n\\times n$ symmetric matrix over $R$, the spectral theorem states the following:\n\n- The eigenvalues of $A$ are all real.\n\n- Eigenvectors corresponding to different eigenvalues are orthogonal.\n\n- The dimension of the eigenspace for each eigenvalue equals the multiplicity of that eigenvalue as a root of the characteristic polynomial.\n\n- $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “$A$ is not diagonalizable” is false. According to the theorem, any symmetric matrix with real entries is orthogonally diagonalizable, hence, is diagonalizable.\n\n- Statement “If $u$ and $v$ are two eigenvectors that correspond to different eigenvalues, then $u⊥v$” is true. Indeed, eigenvectors corresponding to different eigenvalues must be orthogonal.\n\n- Statement “$A$ must have $4$ real (not necessarily distinct) eigenvalues” is true. Indeed, a $4\\times 4$ matrix has $4$ (not necessarily distinct) eigenvalues and the eigenvalues of a symmetric matrix are real.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “If $u$ and $v$ are two eigenvectors that correspond to different eigenvalues, then $u⊥v$”; “$A$ must have $4$ real (not necessarily distinct) eigenvalues” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366650 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162013 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c67d532-acd9-5231-a27f-d39084863e01" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Spectral Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d4d9fb91-ae25-53ed-a33c-52e7f364db71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452209} {:answer/id #uuid "4c814939-1a7e-5031-9e14-64d8c9b51ea6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452210}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4c814939-1a7e-5031-9e14-64d8c9b51ea6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452210} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d68a3af-4038-51ee-9cc7-15a7b64191e6" :answer-field/key "$A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452952} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7914c4bc-6016-5c15-9903-b3e24296b928" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452211} {:answer/id #uuid "98ac60b1-7d42-5198-abee-c2977adfc88a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452212}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7914c4bc-6016-5c15-9903-b3e24296b928" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452211} :answer-field/id #uuid "fa8226b8-812d-57b4-8dff-7d10faebed24" :answer-field/key "$A$ must have $36$ real eigenvalues" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452953} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61ee797c-7e47-51b1-b65a-01a807de3be9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452213} {:answer/id #uuid "ac26379d-5127-5f07-aecb-9307a97b011c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452214}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "61ee797c-7e47-51b1-b65a-01a807de3be9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452213} :answer-field/id #uuid "99678f97-4a7f-57fc-8ce5-ae8e7084ae62" :answer-field/key "If $u$ and $v$ are two eigenvectors, then $u$ is parallel to $v$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452954}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0baadb2d-d441-5a17-add5-c8350f576311" :question/math-academy-id "q-95700" :question/problem "Given that $A$ is a $6\\times 6$ real symmetric matrix, which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Given that $A$ is an $n\\times n$ symmetric matrix over $R$, the spectral theorem states the following:\n\n- The eigenvalues of $A$ are all real.\n\n- Eigenvectors corresponding to different eigenvalues are orthogonal.\n\n- The dimension of the eigenspace for each eigenvalue equals the multiplicity of that eigenvalue as a root of the characteristic polynomial.\n\n- $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “$A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable” is true. Indeed, any symmetric matrix with real entries is orthogonally diagonalizable.\n\n- Statement “$A$ must have $36$ real eigenvalues” is false. According to the spectral theorem, the eigenvalues are real but there are not $36$ of them. An $n\\times n$ matrix has at most $n$ distinct eigenvalues.\n\n- Statement “If $u$ and $v$ are two eigenvectors, then $u$ is parallel to $v$” is false. According to the theorem, eigenvectors corresponding to different eigenvalues must be orthogonal. So if the two eigenvalues correspond to different eigenvalues, they cannot be parallel.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366657 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162013 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c67d532-acd9-5231-a27f-d39084863e01" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Spectral Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a37efe81-e4e1-5a83-93e0-08cabbfe53d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452215} {:answer/id #uuid "2a6cbf3a-43ca-51ee-8eb3-b50854600205" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452216}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a6cbf3a-43ca-51ee-8eb3-b50854600205" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452216} :answer-field/id #uuid "479f0a5a-bddd-55ed-aa58-35002f747d6d" :answer-field/key "If $u$ and $v$ are two eigenvectors that correspond to the same eigenvalue, then $u$ is parallel to $v$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452955} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26c13ebd-2fcf-502b-bb98-1a35a8d3954c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452217} {:answer/id #uuid "9e9346d4-95e8-50e0-a1f6-50b3b049ce5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452218}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e9346d4-95e8-50e0-a1f6-50b3b049ce5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452218} :answer-field/id #uuid "43fd6f8e-d6c4-55b8-83df-719636ccff26" :answer-field/key "$A$ must have $7$ real and distinct eigenvalues" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452956} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01499ffa-8b4c-5e53-aadb-bcf349545ebd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452219} {:answer/id #uuid "a978bc16-54ab-589d-955b-d621b0ea98ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452220}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01499ffa-8b4c-5e53-aadb-bcf349545ebd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452219} :answer-field/id #uuid "a126a47e-711d-55e4-b163-1b04af3cc6c1" :answer-field/key "$A$ is orthogonal" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452957}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "531e02eb-ae49-5805-b616-b28b7ddc3a97" :question/math-academy-id "q-95702" :question/problem "Given that $A$ is a $7\\times 7$ real symmetric matrix whose roots of the characteristic polynomial have multiplicity equal to $1,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Given that $A$ is an $n\\times n$ symmetric matrix over $R$, the spectral theorem states the following:\n\n- The eigenvalues of $A$ are all real.\n\n- Eigenvectors corresponding to different eigenvalues are orthogonal.\n\n- The dimension of the eigenspace for each eigenvalue equals the multiplicity of that eigenvalue as a root of the characteristic polynomial.\n\n- $A$ is orthogonally diagonalizable.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “If $u$ and $v$ are two eigenvectors that correspond to the same eigenvalue, then $u$ is parallel to $v$” is true. According to the theorem, eigenvectors corresponding to different eigenvalues must be orthogonal. But two eigenvectors that correspond to the same eigenvalue will always be parallel if the geometric multiplicity of the eigenvalue is $1.$\n\n- Statement “$A$ must have $7$ real and distinct eigenvalues” is true. Indeed, the eigenvalues of a symmetric matrix are real. Also, since the roots of the characteristic polynomial have (algebraic) multiplicity all equal to $1,$ the eigenvalues are all distinct.\n\n- Statement “$A$ is orthogonal” is false. According to the theorem, any symmetric matrix with real entries is orthogonally diagonalizable, but not necessarily orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “If $u$ and $v$ are two eigenvectors that correspond to the same eigenvalue, then $u$ is parallel to $v$”; “$A$ must have $7$ real and distinct eigenvalues” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366718 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172029 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7a6bbb34-a43f-5e16-8a24-25c0d6970a38" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Quadratic Curve Type From Its Reduced Equation: No Cross-Product Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8bf4e76a-3e69-5df8-aa83-036f966abd9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469800} {:answer/id #uuid "7a49c557-85d3-5090-94ea-4936242de870" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469801}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8bf4e76a-3e69-5df8-aa83-036f966abd9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469800} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac6afe36-c38f-50b2-8661-ed83db10ddea" :answer-field/key "$2{x}_{2}^{2}+1=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470512} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b42ef67-ca74-5b6b-9a27-0020c5e96699" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469802} {:answer/id #uuid "65006734-0d95-5936-a0e3-ef37c8602ed9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469803}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5b42ef67-ca74-5b6b-9a27-0020c5e96699" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469802} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3f1528a-e085-51b6-bebe-f7aa6f28819f" :answer-field/key "$-2{x}_{1}^{2}-3{x}_{2}^{2}=2$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470513} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "413cf20b-5ae7-5ba3-b881-4f921314ed2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469804} {:answer/id #uuid "b7a0c67e-b3f0-5f31-b17c-15e34178268e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469805}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b7a0c67e-b3f0-5f31-b17c-15e34178268e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186469805} :answer-field/id #uuid "a18c0e07-fb20-5a04-bff6-1eb62f90e1ef" :answer-field/key "${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470514}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "66610d66-38e7-5d9e-b883-9467a5f6ad73" :question/math-academy-id "q-95897" :question/problem "Which of the following equations define a non-empty set of points in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$ plane?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each of the equations in turn.\n\n- The equation $2{x}_{2}^{2}+1=0$ defines an empty set. Notice that the left-hand side is positive for all ${x}_{2},$ while the right-hand side is zero.\n\n- The equation $-2{x}_{1}^{2}-3{x}_{2}^{2}=2$ defines an empty set. Notice that the left-hand side is non-positive for all ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2},$ while the right-hand side is  positive.\n\n- The equation ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}=0$ defines a non-empty set in the ${x}_{1}{x}_{2}$-plane. It represents a parabola that opens in the direction of the negative ${x}_{2}$-axis.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}=0$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366739 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162024 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bcd0ca26-3f22-533f-9d08-1533eeddbc79" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Properties of Symmetric Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ed889475-ee9c-533a-8ed7-519387cf84ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452275} {:answer/id #uuid "62ca60ad-f94f-54fa-a2aa-9d8d07b4e4cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452276}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "62ca60ad-f94f-54fa-a2aa-9d8d07b4e4cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452276} :answer-field/id #uuid "c3d3a26b-faad-53a5-854d-c0ce0d455ccd" :answer-field/key "The sum of two $n\\times n$ diagonal matrices is always symmetric." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452985} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ecf353d3-2d53-5e63-8b1b-661cb9cb2eea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452277} {:answer/id #uuid "909b121b-5859-5703-a1e4-479d1c1bdd03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452278}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ecf353d3-2d53-5e63-8b1b-661cb9cb2eea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452277} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1c88bb5-4a8e-5849-bf4a-edd46eb5f6a3" :answer-field/key "If $A$ is *any* $n\\times n$ matrix, then ${A}^{-1}$ is always symmetric (if it exists)." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452986} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "570d980e-c237-5c27-a69f-2dee15bb061a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452279} {:answer/id #uuid "8e4665db-639e-5f41-9895-fb286356fc79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452280}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e4665db-639e-5f41-9895-fb286356fc79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452280} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c01aa01-2f35-5253-9a12-c161f9fc3268" :answer-field/key "If $A$ is *any* matrix, then ${A}^{T}A$ is always symmetric." :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452987}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "df6e5142-313f-5de3-a45d-4da4b737f0bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-96594" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "A square matrix $A$ is symmetric if it is equal to its transpose:\n\n$$\nA={A}^{T}\n$$\n\nEquivalently, ${a}_{ij}={a}_{ji}$ for all $i,j.$ In other words, $A$ is symmetric across its main diagonal.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “The sum of two $n\\times n$ diagonal matrices is always symmetric” is true. Indeed, for any diagonal matrices $A$ and $B,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(A+B)^{T} & ={A}^{T}+{B}^{T} \\\\  & =A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “If $A$ is *any* $n\\times n$ matrix, then ${A}^{-1}$ is always symmetric (if it exists)” is false. As a counterexample, consider the non-singular matrix \n\n$\\,$ $A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.$\n\nHowever, ${A}^{-1}$ is not symmetric:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{-1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “If $A$ is *any* matrix, then ${A}^{T}A$ is always symmetric” is true. Indeed, for any $m\\times n$ matrix $A,$ we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({A}^{T}A)^{T} & ={A}^{T}({A}^{T})^{T} \\\\  & ={A}^{T}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “The sum of two $n\\times n$ diagonal matrices is always symmetric”; “If $A$ is *any* matrix, then ${A}^{T}A$ is always symmetric” only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366773 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176089 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba9d2401-4e30-5327-b8eb-b4db7108dd0c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Linear Transformations in the Cartesian Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9099ef15-a1bf-5b8d-b674-d360c6aadda0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452311} {:answer/id #uuid "8e1abad4-8933-51f0-817c-845930bc0ec6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452312}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e1abad4-8933-51f0-817c-845930bc0ec6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452312} :answer-field/id #uuid "c524b1b4-bbda-5e7e-890d-f074894a05f4" :answer-field/key "$⟨-1,0⟩\\in \\text{Im}(T)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453003} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ddf10a9-2422-536a-afd4-fa994f101802" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452313} {:answer/id #uuid "245d7eb5-f7c7-52bc-aee9-07abe4358c15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452314}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "245d7eb5-f7c7-52bc-aee9-07abe4358c15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452314} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2341323-e8a8-593f-ac40-f2d6042f60fe" :answer-field/key "$\\text{Ker}(T)={0}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453004} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f1a969b-4d20-56b8-a302-390ca21fb96c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452315} {:answer/id #uuid "38ab8235-cad9-5536-9cf5-1a513f15a9d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452316}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f1a969b-4d20-56b8-a302-390ca21fb96c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452315} :answer-field/id #uuid "57938303-0bc4-5294-929a-a9e479e34260" :answer-field/key "$\\text{nullity}(T)=2$, where $T$ is the standard matrix of $T$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453005}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cbb0c470-3f82-5cd2-8de9-680715504460" :question/math-academy-id "q-97077" :question/problem "Consider a linear transformation $T$ that maps ${R}^{2}$ onto  ${R}^{2}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that a linear transformation $T$ maps ${R}^{2}$ onto  ${R}^{2}$ if and only if \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)=2\n$$\n\nand, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{nullity}(T) & =2-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =2-2 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement $⟨-1,0⟩\\in \\text{Im}(T)$ is true. Indeed, The condition $\\text{rank}(T)=2$ in ${R}^{2}$ is equivalent to \n\n$$\n\\text{Im}(T)={R}^{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, $T$ is surjective and so  $⟨-1,0⟩\\in \\text{Im}(T).$\n\n- Statement $\\text{Ker}(T)={0}$ is true. Indeed, the condition $\\text{nullity}(T)=0$ in ${R}^{2}$ is equivalent to \n\n$$\n\\text{Ker}(T)={0}.\n$$\n\n- Statement “$\\text{nullity}(T)=2$, where $T$ is the standard matrix of $T$” is false. We have that \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(T)=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $⟨-1,0⟩\\in \\text{Im}(T)$; $\\text{Ker}(T)={0}$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366780 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176089 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba9d2401-4e30-5327-b8eb-b4db7108dd0c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Linear Transformations in the Cartesian Plane"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12eb12db-c865-5a15-995f-57d1e99856b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452323} {:answer/id #uuid "fa2992f7-296f-52e8-8e2f-b1875fa14a31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452324}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "12eb12db-c865-5a15-995f-57d1e99856b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452323} :answer-field/id #uuid "12ebab7b-febe-5850-8be3-97869ab43f9b" :answer-field/key "$⟨2,1⟩\\in \\text{Im}(T)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453009} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dff232c6-f90e-549a-bf5d-1544c0d7eb35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452325} {:answer/id #uuid "cc22eb64-3d36-5f56-935a-3d6d0d508e49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452326}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cc22eb64-3d36-5f56-935a-3d6d0d508e49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452326} :answer-field/id #uuid "9583cd23-de18-5d81-9215-33038a6df612" :answer-field/key "$\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453010} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d092dba5-3f4c-5126-93c1-b72bf8d37079" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452327} {:answer/id #uuid "3d4acc6c-2043-51df-a00b-c80988412bef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452328}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d092dba5-3f4c-5126-93c1-b72bf8d37079" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186452327} :answer-field/id #uuid "5957978d-2e1f-5607-8d63-c7b81149356e" :answer-field/key "$\\text{nullity}(T)=2$, where $T$ is the standard matrix of $T$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453011}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2335439b-17b4-5a3d-b1ce-91063205bf0d" :question/math-academy-id "q-97079" :question/problem "Consider a linear transformation $T$ that maps ${R}^{2}$ onto the line $y=2x.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Recall that when the image of a linear transformation $T$ in ${R}^{2}$ with the standard matrix $T$ is a one-dimensional line, then\n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)=1,\n$$\n\nand, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T)=\\text{nullity}(T) & =2-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =2-1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement $⟨2,1⟩\\in \\text{Im}(T)$ is false. The point $(2,1)$ does not satisfy the equation of the line $y=2x$ since $1\\ne 2\\cdot 2.$ Hence, \n\n$$\n⟨2,1⟩\\notin \\text{Im}(T).\n$$\n\n- Statement $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}$ is true. Since $\\text{nullity}(T)=1,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}.\n$$\n\n- Statement “$\\text{nullity}(T)=2$, where $T$ is the standard matrix of $T$” is false. We have that \n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(T)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366794 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059848 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "32df8909-a239-5167-b8ea-412f7cdaea93" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Singular Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c502c08e-cbb1-5442-bcb9-424b6777ac3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438664} {:answer/id #uuid "003139e8-881a-52b3-9ea9-221baca3d942" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438665}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c502c08e-cbb1-5442-bcb9-424b6777ac3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438664} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee174a06-895c-5583-812c-7c4468fa2511" :answer-field/key "$det(D)=1$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439361} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4493f073-130a-5643-b440-425f3034460d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438666} {:answer/id #uuid "0508c95e-9b72-5cb3-8464-ae610809503a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438667}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0508c95e-9b72-5cb3-8464-ae610809503a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438667} :answer-field/id #uuid "495bc1a5-e85e-5043-b4d4-3fe8b15554ed" :answer-field/key "$D$ is a singular matrix" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439362} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b1502369-e88d-503e-90f4-0f6ab218c61c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438668} {:answer/id #uuid "516ece6f-6106-541c-abfc-8c583469231d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438669}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "516ece6f-6106-541c-abfc-8c583469231d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438669} :answer-field/id #uuid "e222facc-8617-59fb-b0f3-b2ffe6c181c7" :answer-field/key "$det(D)=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439363}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2e3acb77-06f5-5592-8e02-3140d23a412e" :question/math-academy-id "q-9835" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the matrix $D$ given below?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n$$\nD=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 6 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that a square matrix is singular if its determinant is equal to $0.$\n\nUsing the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(D) & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 6 & 7\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1|\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ 6 & 7\\end{aligned}|-2|\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 7\\end{aligned}|+3|\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 6\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(28-30)-2(7-5)+3(6-4) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (-2)-2\\cdot 2+3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-2-4+6 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $det(D)=0$, the matrix is singular. \n\nTherefore, only statements “$D$ is a singular matrix” and $det(D)=0$ are true."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366945 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115334 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bf19e2ee-7c42-58b0-9254-5a44f315288a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Block Diagonal and Block Triangular Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3ead9eb7-6179-5dd3-bb4d-a0b6266823fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468870} {:answer/id #uuid "8b9840e8-0b71-5152-86ba-99ddfd860c5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468871}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3ead9eb7-6179-5dd3-bb4d-a0b6266823fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468870} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea3fe40b-78a5-502f-88f4-ea17a1a7eed1" :answer-field/key "$A=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 9 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470047} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63f648f4-6b6b-54ca-b233-6007032093f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468872} {:answer/id #uuid "3b188c3a-165e-5a4d-8e93-a4c74af56e13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468873}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63f648f4-6b6b-54ca-b233-6007032093f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468872} :answer-field/id #uuid "bac8d59a-f4f8-59b0-a422-e8fcb6fc24d7" :answer-field/key "$B=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 8 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470048} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b5d9824e-7b54-553e-bf8d-b4f60f9d4401" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468874} {:answer/id #uuid "ad3e8787-8c89-5c14-83be-c185f11748cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468875}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ad3e8787-8c89-5c14-83be-c185f11748cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468875} :answer-field/id #uuid "3202f73f-6a44-5bd9-9c2c-4f8276a03317" :answer-field/key "$C=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470049}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3da29d09-2c10-59b3-aafb-a3f2d64ea706" :question/math-academy-id "q-109246" :question/problem "Which of the following matrices are block upper triangular?\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "A block upper triangular matrix can be partitioned so that it has only square blocks on the main diagonal, and the blocks below the main diagonal contain only zeros.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our matrices.\n\n- $A$is *not* a block upper triangular matrix since it can't be partitioned in the way described above:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 9 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n- $B$ is *not* a block upper triangular matrix since it can't be partitioned in the way described above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 8 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 8 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 8 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- $C$ is a block upper triangular matrix. Indeed, we can partition it as follows:\n\n$$\nC=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$C$ only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367338 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152193 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0829d4a-239a-5a8e-9c8e-3cdc76a993bc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions of Linear ODEs Using the Constant Multiple Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb980002-d829-5bac-a9f4-693f673bde2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468918} {:answer/id #uuid "82d66952-0268-575c-82cb-30e16ee2d2c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468919}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82d66952-0268-575c-82cb-30e16ee2d2c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468919} :answer-field/id #uuid "8021bc97-48f3-5ab9-9217-1f692dc8e065" :answer-field/key "$y(x)={e}^{4-2x}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470071} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "527df5f6-a00e-5cb4-9a6a-439df57713f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468920} {:answer/id #uuid "98419330-8a81-5717-a937-ca1110ba6085" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468921}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "527df5f6-a00e-5cb4-9a6a-439df57713f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468920} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a4d18f3-bd26-5c4d-a55c-a3b096153545" :answer-field/key "$y(x)=x{e}^{-2x}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470072} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "89da5af0-e85d-5122-9bbe-4d6743db2cb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468922} {:answer/id #uuid "cedf372f-ea7d-5fc6-8e97-1703247cb294" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468923}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cedf372f-ea7d-5fc6-8e97-1703247cb294" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468923} :answer-field/id #uuid "72714db6-c639-5979-9a48-742baf128ee3" :answer-field/key "$y(x)=\\frac{2}{{e}^{2x}}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470073}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5c98c0e9-7419-5d7e-b919-df2475fb8298" :question/math-academy-id "q-131025" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-2x}$ is a solution to the equation $(1+2x){y}^{″}+4x{y}^{′}-4y=0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the function ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and $c$ is a constant, then $y(x)=c{y}_{1}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)={e}^{4-2x}={e}^{4}\\cdot {e}^{-2x}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c={e}^{4}.$\n\n- The function $y(x)=x{e}^{-2x}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of a non-constant function and a known solution.\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\frac{2}{{e}^{2x}}=2\\cdot {e}^{-2x}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=2.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $y(x)={e}^{4-2x}$; $y(x)=\\frac{2}{{e}^{2x}}$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367345 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152193 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0829d4a-239a-5a8e-9c8e-3cdc76a993bc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions of Linear ODEs Using the Constant Multiple Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "016b1440-004a-5e39-8d68-b109db73255c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468924} {:answer/id #uuid "628f0776-ec70-5711-b2f5-0c5156869c39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468925}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "628f0776-ec70-5711-b2f5-0c5156869c39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468925} :answer-field/id #uuid "35695d2a-47ab-5e02-95e6-7e73b87bee03" :answer-field/key "$y(x)={e}^{(x-3)/3}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470074} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a58cccc-4f85-589b-9846-47b6f32f47b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468926} {:answer/id #uuid "aace0540-eb13-5045-a32c-23e9f4d5cd50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468927}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a58cccc-4f85-589b-9846-47b6f32f47b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468926} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a0bffd9-93be-5968-a9e9-db0930d4f4d4" :answer-field/key "$y(x)=\\frac{x}{3}{e}^{x/3}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470075} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80f02ff4-30f9-57c7-81a9-0b6859391ae8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468928} {:answer/id #uuid "ccea8c3f-2ba0-5435-ae58-7f06be3c8f08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468929}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ccea8c3f-2ba0-5435-ae58-7f06be3c8f08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468929} :answer-field/id #uuid "699db024-2862-5b7b-9d8e-321a8c84be8c" :answer-field/key "$y(x)=\\frac{5{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x/3}}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470076}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e6f1fe4c-12f4-51c1-8c80-ca5bb6fcfc47" :question/math-academy-id "q-131027" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x/3}$ is a solution to the equation $6{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}-y=0,$ then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the function ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and $c$ is a constant, then $y(x)=c{y}_{1}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)={e}^{(x-3)/3}={e}^{-1}\\cdot {e}^{x/3}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c={e}^{-1}.$\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\frac{x}{3}{e}^{x/3}$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of a non-constant function and a known solution.\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\frac{5{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x/3}}=5\\cdot {e}^{x-2x/3}=5{e}^{x/3}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=5.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $y(x)={e}^{(x-3)/3}$; $y(x)=\\frac{5{e}^{x}}{{e}^{2x/3}}$ only."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367352 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152169 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "101e0c22-fbb4-5865-af5f-d669e2488d09" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions to Linear ODEs Using the Superposition Principle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a110499-d964-53fd-b1cc-a6e49131c202" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468930} {:answer/id #uuid "59842690-4580-59b5-a6ed-d947ae64b3c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468931}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "59842690-4580-59b5-a6ed-d947ae64b3c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468931} :answer-field/id #uuid "72865be2-5ec6-5323-ae78-ecc14e35f121" :answer-field/key "$y(t)=2{e}^{-3t}+3{e}^{-2t}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470077} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d122ddec-72db-59a3-9fe1-bc707e84fc32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468932} {:answer/id #uuid "bba2240b-c719-5668-8120-54329467c641" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468933}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bba2240b-c719-5668-8120-54329467c641" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468933} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b3790d9-1a04-59a1-bfff-e050665e8224" :answer-field/key "$y(t)=(4+{e}^{t}){e}^{-3t}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470078} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "983ad9d1-7773-5bfc-ba86-dd47a61a4b40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468934} {:answer/id #uuid "a28122a7-e37c-5832-948b-fc6df880d5ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468935}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a28122a7-e37c-5832-948b-fc6df880d5ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186468935} :answer-field/id #uuid "96f2fbcf-6a92-57fa-b4e5-a9504d0e83ee" :answer-field/key "$y(t)=5{e}^{-3t}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470079}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5af618e0-efed-5d52-a5fe-f92221b7400c" :question/math-academy-id "q-131038" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(t)={e}^{-3t}$ and ${y}_{2}(t)={e}^{-2t}$ are solutions to the equation ${y}^{″}+5{y}^{′}+6y=0,$ which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(t)$ and ${y}_{2}(t)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ are constants, then $y(t)={c}_{1}{y}_{1}(t)+{c}_{2}{y}_{2}(t)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nWe're given that ${y}_{1}(t)={e}^{-3t}$ and ${y}_{2}(t)={e}^{-2t}.$ Therefore, the following function is also a solution:\n\n$$\ny(t)={c}_{1}{e}^{-3t}+{c}_{2}t{e}^{-2t}\n$$\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(t)=2{e}^{-3t}+3{e}^{-2t}$ is a solution of the given equation. Here, ${c}_{1}=2,{c}_{2}=3.$\n\n- The function $y(t)=(4+{e}^{t}){e}^{-3t}=4{e}^{-3t}+{e}^{-2t}$ is a solution of the given equation. Here, ${c}_{1}=4,{c}_{2}=1.$\n\n- The function $y(t)=5{e}^{-3t}$ is a solution of the given equation. Here, ${c}_{1}=5,{c}_{2}=0.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367373 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124480 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b1a557d6-52f3-5df3-8088-5b875296e72e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Pivot Columns of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41813e56-ee59-548f-94a4-503af2000ee6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186574327} {:answer/id #uuid "c10408e1-a7a9-5021-80a1-5b1136af118a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186574328}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "41813e56-ee59-548f-94a4-503af