b/id #edb/ref 17592186471080}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d03f060-6ebf-5283-a886-1008c9f73000" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471079} :answer-field/id #uuid "40d38ef1-5e92-5126-b1e0-b9dca3c1fa9f" :answer-field/key "$Π$ is closed under scalar multiplication" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472045} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85d0b485-371d-58e9-a8c2-75e1166ae969" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471081} {:answer/id #uuid "5634bb41-dbfb-535e-8b35-09eb6c38952c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471082}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "85d0b485-371d-58e9-a8c2-75e1166ae969" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471081} :answer-field/id #uuid "894f59eb-e3c4-5a32-80d8-8b6c6c7a4fbb" :answer-field/key "$Π$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472046}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b6fdf40e-16e2-5d4d-87e5-0c2a5c46b067" :question/math-academy-id "q-157475" :question/problem "Let $Π$ be the plane with equation $3x+2y+z=1$ in ${R}^{3}.$ Which of the following statements are true? \n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "Recall the following two facts about subspaces: \n\n- A set of vectors in a vector space is a *subspace* if it is closed under vector addition and scalar multiplication.\n\n- If a non-empty set is closed under scalar multiplication, then this set must contain the zero-vector (because $0\\cdot v=0$ for any vector $v$). So, if a set does not contain the zero vector, then this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\nNotice that $Π$ contains vectors of the form \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y \\\\ 1-3x-2y\\end{bmatrix},\\,x,y\\in R.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements. \n\n- Statement “$Π$ contains the zero-vector” is false. The line does not pass through the origin since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+2y+z & =1 \\\\ 3(0)+2(0)+(0) & =1 \\\\ 0 & =1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$Π$ is closed under vector addition” is false. Let's consider $a=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\in Π$ and $b=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\in Π.$ Then \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a+b & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0+1 \\\\ 0+(-1) \\\\ 1+0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ does not belong to $Π$ since \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+2y+z & =1 \\\\ 3(1)+2(-1)+(1) & =1 \\\\ 2 & =1\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$Π$ is closed under scalar multiplication” is false. Since $0\\notin Π,$ this set is not closed under scalar multiplication.\n\n- Statement “$Π$ is a subspace of ${R}^{3}$” is false. Since statements “$Π$ is closed under vector addition” and “$Π$ is closed under scalar multiplication” are false, $L$ is *not* a subspace of ${R}^{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376075 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105861 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "16a6f0f7-4003-5908-8b1b-68d218fdcb8f" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Function Is a Valid PDF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "90dcc132-8806-58fb-a61c-c95866435d63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471083} {:answer/id #uuid "a05fbe18-c3e4-5725-92fa-ba8d739ac71f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471084}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a05fbe18-c3e4-5725-92fa-ba8d739ac71f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471084} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a594e37-46ef-5d1a-a4b8-4c13868d3694" :answer-field/key "$f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472047} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8de8aee8-e7ae-5809-8bc3-2c539b8dd567" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471085} {:answer/id #uuid "7b060391-9171-5280-8d27-3a79c55dea31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471086}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7b060391-9171-5280-8d27-3a79c55dea31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471086} :answer-field/id #uuid "dfb01ced-752c-59fa-9642-732e03a75fa1" :answer-field/key "${\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472048} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a6849ec8-7cdf-569c-a934-902d354767eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471087} {:answer/id #uuid "c817c900-ca18-5d1e-9e95-3c8c1d11b310" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471088}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c817c900-ca18-5d1e-9e95-3c8c1d11b310" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471088} :answer-field/id #uuid "172b3a05-bb98-5af8-9278-d9fa53e3c4cd" :answer-field/key "$f(x)$ is a valid probability density function" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472049}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cced02f8-7b29-5540-80d2-baca7e098b0d" :question/math-academy-id "q-158417" :question/problem "![](images/52/52cb038e475b4d0d5de64684340e7291e0c8cd82072f2f30a1c4025af69cc809.png)\n\nConsider the function $f(x)$ whose graph is shown above.\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}2x,\\, & 0\\le x<1, \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\nIf none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." :question/worked-solution "For a function $f(x)$ to be a valid probability density function on a set $S,$ it must satisfy the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in S$\n\n- ${\\int }_{S}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$\n\nNow, let's consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement “$f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$” is true. From the graph, we see that $f(x)$ is nonnegative for all $x.$\n\n- Statement ${\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$ is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[{1}^{2}]-[{0}^{2}] \\\\  & =1-0 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement “$f(x)$ is a valid probability density function” is true. Since statements “$f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$” and ${\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$ are true, $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is “$f(x)\\ge 0$ for all $x$”; ${\\int }_{0}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=1$; “$f(x)$ is a valid probability density function”."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376082 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124641 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "64ba57cc-a3eb-582a-963b-b94895a20bac" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points From Linear Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f9d5b57d-d397-5e8b-9a46-ca703dc128d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453430} {:answer/id #uuid "654fdd13-26b0-5b13-a201-e47a6bbd127f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453431}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "654fdd13-26b0-5b13-a201-e47a6bbd127f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453431} :answer-field/id #uuid "f245c56b-ea24-5bc6-9714-193572353072" :answer-field/key "The point $P(2,4)$ lies on the boundary of $A$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454465} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c17641c7-2616-5f44-913a-204e61d02795" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453432} {:answer/id #uuid "1798276f-6c7e-5444-91e4-9f13f58ce650" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453433}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1798276f-6c7e-5444-91e4-9f13f58ce650" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453433} :answer-field/id #uuid "2595354c-4c90-5eb8-bc56-8aa27847f7cd" :answer-field/key "The point $P(2,4)$ belongs to $A$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454466} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7f28fa17-78af-5e9c-9940-0619d6581c75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453434} {:answer/id #uuid "03c6eb37-ee8b-5456-b1b0-d792a68992e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453435}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f28fa17-78af-5e9c-9940-0619d6581c75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453434} :answer-field/id #uuid "55044069-05bd-53df-bf38-5763c4c860a7" :answer-field/key "The point $Q(-2,3)$ is an interior point of $A$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454467}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "80400990-b978-5e6c-95c6-54cde07862e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-158534" :question/problem "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y\\le x+2},\\,∂A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y=x+2}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie on or below the line $y=x+2.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/3b/3b0505f0c1fb295665dd64c9d0f26f23960b638ef0e1d414c7ad306a89154dfb.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(2,4)$ and $Q(-2,3)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/62/623f00c423c7a846c52537d58c071c730083ba963fb488dbedbb76637ea9cc8c.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “The point $P(2,4)$ lies on the boundary of $A$” is true. The coordinates of $P(2,4)$ satisfy the equation $y=x+2.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement “The point $P(2,4)$ belongs to $A$” is true. The coordinates of $P(2,4)$ satisfy the inequality $y\\le x+4.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement “The point $Q(-2,3)$ is an interior point of $A$” is false. Indeed, $Q$ is not an interior point of $A$ since any neighborhood of $Q$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is '“The point $P(2,4)$ lies on the boundary of $A$” and “The point $P(2,4)$ belongs to $A$” only.'"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376089 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124641 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "64ba57cc-a3eb-582a-963b-b94895a20bac" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points From Linear Inequalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c2a38983-2ac2-5747-84de-52654204b669" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453442} {:answer/id #uuid "24fa1281-9195-576c-958f-f5a1cd7756b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453443}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24fa1281-9195-576c-958f-f5a1cd7756b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453443} :answer-field/id #uuid "b974481d-3120-5f01-a603-07099f42a784" :answer-field/key "The point $P(-2,1)$ lies on the boundary of $A$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454471} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5f4a6e95-fed8-5f60-b3be-40a55090ad74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453444} {:answer/id #uuid "0fc66c24-39c8-55f8-949a-fd3c22a45e0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453445}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5f4a6e95-fed8-5f60-b3be-40a55090ad74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453444} :answer-field/id #uuid "4ab5754e-a652-5899-87ab-53e612a79fd5" :answer-field/key "The point $P(-2,1)$ belongs to $A$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454472} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c545dbcc-3d3d-5d7f-8c9e-39f107e5337c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453446} {:answer/id #uuid "7b177e0f-f566-534e-911c-092b634ea208" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453447}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c545dbcc-3d3d-5d7f-8c9e-39f107e5337c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186453446} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ac4f957-7cd7-55eb-a39f-2fc8342eb8a5" :answer-field/key "The point $Q(-2,3)$ is an interior point of $A$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186454473}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7257b8a6-8ea5-5027-98d4-5845f1b59c79" :question/math-academy-id "q-158548" :question/problem "The set $A$ and its boundary $∂A$ are given by\n\n$$\nA={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y<x+3},\\,∂A={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:y=x+3}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is less than $\\delta .$\n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nIn two-dimensional space, a neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is the open disc $|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta ,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/50/50a6c72e169f3104eb0b9958673cc6644f770e6bcc3ebf23360eb20189325a28.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nNow, the set $A$ consists of all points that lie below the line $y=x+3.$ A sketch of this region is shown below:\n\n![](images/7a/7a23cbece856b7b842d5907665af5ea593a06faea79283b1be13fc4382e0bf26.png)\n\nLet's add the points $P(-2,1)$ and $Q(-2,3)$ and some neighborhoods of these points to our diagram.\n\n![](images/82/82c921570bbc1ce700db5472871048c14c67df7d6895962f87454c70fc1ce192.png)\n\nLet's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “The point $P(-2,1)$ lies on the boundary of $A$” is true. The coordinates of $P(-2,1)$ satisfy $y=x+3.$ Hence, $P$ lies in the set $∂A,$ the boundary of $A.$\n\n- Statement “The point $P(-2,1)$ belongs to $A$” is false. The coordinates of $P(-2,1)$ do not satisfy the inequality $y<x+3.$ Hence, $P$ does not lie in the set $A.$\n\n- Statement “The point $Q(-2,3)$ is an interior point of $A$” is false. Indeed, $Q$ is not an interior point of $A$ since any neighborhood of $Q$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is '“The point $P(-2,1)$ lies on the boundary of $A$” only.'"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376096 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124635 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b34c9c06-e884-5758-a184-39330a07d8cd" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points of Sets of Real Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ff2597b-4563-52dd-97bd-79931863e5bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471089} {:answer/id #uuid "c64121cc-495f-5d02-8baa-6347a5757f61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471090}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c64121cc-495f-5d02-8baa-6347a5757f61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471090} :answer-field/id #uuid "e5738039-4384-58fe-ba54-1ac5ac80b370" :answer-field/key "$A$ is an interior point of the set $M$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472050} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ba8d4db-3185-546a-9558-c0532b074b4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471091} {:answer/id #uuid "c5c061e7-01b4-514b-badc-0fe24712237a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471092}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c5c061e7-01b4-514b-badc-0fe24712237a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471092} :answer-field/id #uuid "47c6223b-9267-5508-b98c-7c2d2e8d01e6" :answer-field/key "$B$ is a boundary point of the set $M$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472051} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "668d955d-390f-5905-adc9-a8877fa264d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471093} {:answer/id #uuid "e62939fe-e47a-5ae6-8bda-ff784b7b4a09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471094}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "668d955d-390f-5905-adc9-a8877fa264d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471093} :answer-field/id #uuid "197eb59a-025c-5dfb-8852-a076b6a377f3" :answer-field/key "$C$ is an interior point of the set $M$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472052}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "82163da2-d426-5778-8627-90a3d54226a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-159253" :question/problem "![](images/b3/b3ea4dd786ab1ff777c7667bb94df36e2c04ceaefba8e4506754d1ec79d03641.png)\n\nConsider the set $M=[-2,0)\\cup (0,2]$ and the points $A,$ $B,$ and $C,$ shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let $\\delta >0.$ A *neighborhood* of a point ${x}_{0}\\in R$ is the set of points whose distance from ${x}_{0}$ is *smaller than* $\\delta :$ \n\n$$\n{x:|x-{x}_{0}|<\\delta }\n$$\n\nA neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ is given by the open interval $({x}_{0}-\\delta ,{x}_{0}+\\delta ),$ shown below.\n\n![](images/f8/f889352f25e7936d0e5ea76585a979efbb301da61f7e99a937e979af93ee0664.png)\n\nFurthermore, we have the following definitions:\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is an *interior point* of a set $S$ if $S$ contains *some* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}.$ The set of all interior points of $S$ is called the *interior* of $S.$\n\n- A point ${x}_{0}$ is a *boundary point* of a set $S$ if *every* neighborhood of ${x}_{0}$ contains points that are in $S$ and points that are not in $S.$ \nThe set of all boundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement “$A$ is an interior point of the set $M$” is true. The point $A$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $A$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\n![](images/c9/c9acfd3e5e7d179904ba69d3473e5f8644055e777fa679e665cfb9199793d218.png)\n\n- Statement “$B$ is a boundary point of the set $M$” is true. The point $B$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $B$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$ Notice that $B$ does not belong to $M$ and $B$ belongs to any open neighborhood of $B.$\n\n![](images/74/7480978db0850ff426ed4747ee7dfb81781ad7222f97fca0d6106c800d99fcbf.png)\n\n- Statement “$C$ is an interior point of the set $M$” is false. The point $C$ is *not* an interior point of $M$ since any neighborhood of $C$ contains at least one point *outside* the set.\n\n![](images/4f/4fb2567d76963e586370ee771f359b3ba051941d650d9d1f0dc58f47bb770ab4.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is '“$A$ is an interior point of the set $M$” and “$B$ is a boundary point of the set $M$” only.'"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376103 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124635 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b34c9c06-e884-5758-a184-39330a07d8cd" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Interior and Boundary Points of Sets of Real Numbers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9eb02c31-7aaa-5f27-b88d-0bac5e5ad915" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/r