oundary points of $S$ is called the *boundary* of $S.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n![](images/4b/4b89766f6ee2794b18a7fa5dc549de62df410942cf68cac9629dd696e8f0d906.png)\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n- Statement “$A$ is a boundary point of the set $M$” is true. Indeed, $A$ is a boundary point because every open neighborhood of $A$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$\n\n- Statement “$B$ is an interior point of the set $M$” is false. Every open neighborhood of $B$ contains points that belong to $M$ and points that do not belong to $M.$ The point $B$ is a boundary point for the given set.\n\n- Statement “$C$ is an interior point of the set $M$” is true. Indeed, $C$ is an interior point since there exists an open neighborhood of $C$ that is fully contained inside the set $M.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is '“$A$ is a boundary point of the set $M$” and “$C$ is an interior point of the set $M$” only'."}]
